A Docker Registry is a stateless, highly scalable server-side application that stores and lets you distribute Docker images. 
It acts as a central repository where you push images after building them and pull them when deploying.

Core Workflow 🔄
Login: docker login <registry_url>
Tag: docker tag <local_image> <registry_url>/<repo_name>:<tag>
Push: docker push <registry_url>/<repo_name>:<tag>
Pull: docker pull <registry_url>/<repo_name>:<tag>

Running a Private Registry
You can host a basic private registry locally with a single command:
docker run -d -p 5000:5000 --name my-registry registry:2

Commands:

ubuntu@ip-172-31-87-248:~projects/two-tier-flask-app$docker system prune        #deletes all unused containers        
ubuntu@ip-172-31-87-248:~projects/two-tier-flask-app$docker rmi <image_ids>     #deletes  image ids
ubuntu@ip-172-31-87-248:~projects/two-tier-flask-app$docker images -aq           #lists ids of all images
ubuntu@ip-172-31-87-248:~projects/two-tier-flask-app$docker rmi -f $(docker images -aq) # removes forcefully images of all stopped containers
ubuntu@ip-172-31-87-248:~projects/two-tier-flask-app$docker compose down        #stops and removes containers

ubuntu@ip-172-31-87-248:~projects/two-tier-flask-app$docker compose up -d --build      #restarts and builds again

If building new docker images for registry, always use docker_username/docker_image_name

Ctrl+c will gracefully shut down the container after docker compose up

